<section><h2>Five practical examples of the compatibility screen</h2>
<h3>Example 1 — An early E46 with a missing body-module connection</h3><p>An E46 owner may find that one diagnostic application identifies the engine but cannot see a body-related control unit. This does not automatically prove an ECU failure or that a newer tablet would work. The ProTool publisher describes earlier chassis that need a double K-line bridge. Before buying a replacement cable, confirm the exact model, installed system and documented wiring/interface requirement. A safe test begins by preserving the original diagnostic observation and determining whether other relevant controllers communicate. It does not involve shorting connector pins by trial and error or attempting write functions until the correct factory procedure is known.</p>
<h3>Example 2 — An F30 user considering wired ENET for BimmerCode</h3><p>The target should be the exact coding option, not the Ethernet link itself. BimmerCode lists supported ENET routes and offers model-specific coding options and control-unit information. Check that the operator's phone or computer can establish the Ethernet transport documented by the app and that the selected F-series vehicle offers the intended function. If the option is absent in the supported selector, buying a faster cable will not create the underlying ECU capability. The app's preparation and safety instructions take precedence over generic retailer setup steps.</p>
<h3>Example 3 — A newer G-series and an apparently supported Bluetooth adapter</h3><p>BimmerCode warns that some G-series modules are accessible only with specific adapters. That means an adapter that works for one G-series task may not meet a second ECU-level requirement on the same vehicle. Separate the generic BLE pairing claim from the exact-control-unit support claim. A useful decision record keeps the chassis, selected coding option, app version and adapter revision together. If uncertainty persists, regard that combination as unverified, not a reason to try expert-level coding with unsupported equipment.</p>
<h3>Example 4 — An independent workshop evaluating ISTA equipment</h3><p>BMW's AOS requirements identify approved vehicle communication options and a separate authorized software environment. A workshop selecting hardware for diagnosis or programming must evaluate access, software version, interface model, local IT constraints and power support. The fact that a consumer ENET cable works in a mobile app is not evidence that it satisfies those professional requirements. Document the exact intended BMW procedure and verify the AOS system requirements rather than projecting one vehicle-generation rule onto a whole workshop fleet.</p>
<h3>Example 5 — An R-series MINI owner with a scanner already on hand</h3><p>Start with the MINI's exact generation and the target module. A tool capable of standard emissions diagnostics might not offer complete service or coding support. Check the developer's MINI-specific vehicle coverage, approved connection type and system availability. Avoid inferring from a mechanically similar BMW E-series that the same adapter and app entitlement will behave identically. When diagnostics involve brakes, airbags or another safety-critical system, use the proper service reference and qualified escalation.</p>
</section>